    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "The unpredicted, complex nature of earthquakes such as the M7.3 June 28 1992 Landers earthquake, which did not rupture the extent of any one previously mapped fault, but N and NW across segments of four sub-parallel, NW-SE trending faults, proves difficult to understand and thus difficult to incorporate into probability models used to mitigate seismic hazard. This multi-fault rupture is not unique: other earthquakes have exhibited similar behavior including the 2002 Denali, Alaska, earthquake that ruptured parts of three different faults (Eberhart-Phillips et al 2003). This has led to efforts to characterize statistically when ruptures jump from one fault to another (e.g. Black and Jackson 2008, Wesnousky 2008), but attention to the influence of 3D fault structure is minimal despite research demonstrating its importance in earthquake rupture (e.g. Aochi and Fukuyama 2002, Harris 2004). This work outlines a method for analyzing the correspondence between geologic and geophysical data associated with the Landers earthquake. As part of this effort, quasi-static models built separately from these sets of data will illuminate the relationship between surface and subsurface activity at Landers. Modeled stress change distributions and displacements will provide insight into this relationship and the role of fault structure in the complex rupture geometry.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
